    <div class="frame">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">19</span>Lab A1b</p>
      <h2 class="h2">Scope by <span class="accent">exclusion</span>.</h2>

      <div class="frame-body">
      <div class="labcard" style="margin-top: 18px;">
        <span class="lab-tag">Lab A1b: ignore the noise</span>
        <h3>Let the agent explore, but fence off dead weight</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 23px; color: var(--fg-muted); margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Objective:</strong> the repo now carries a <code>src/legacy/</code> folder of plausible but irrelevant session and auth files. Unscoped, the agent reads them on false leads and the baseline climbs. Block that folder and run the same task.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Add an ignore rule that fences off <code>src/legacy/</code>, then run the same symptom prompt with no file names.
            <div class="prompt-block">Claude Code &middot; <code>.claude/settings.json</code><br>{ "permissions": { "deny": ["Read(./src/legacy/**)"] } }</div>
          </li>
          <li>Same idea in the other tools: <code>src/legacy/</code> in <code>.cursorignore</code> (Cursor), or a content-exclusion path (Copilot).</li>
          <li>Record the tokens and compare against your noisy Lab 0 baseline.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="lab-out"><strong>Expected result:</strong> close to the scoped Lab A1 number, with the same correct fix, because the agent never wanders into the decoys.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        Inclusion picks the few right files. Exclusion removes the obvious wrong ones. Reach for exclusion when you cannot list every file up front.
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
